%% Workflow schema: what calls what, for the two commands that matter.
%% The boxes are a function-level graph of one run: the functions these two
%% commands actually call, plus the child-process entry points
%% (shard_worker.main, stage_runner.main). The functions of the six essential
%% modules are a subset of them, 25 of the 49 function boxes; the other 24
%% belong to 19 further modules of the package plus one repository script.
%% This departs from the standard's rule "boxes = the six essential modules"
%% on purpose: six boxes cannot show what calls what inside the flow.
%% Two module counts, because they are different numbers. Modules drawn: 25
%% package modules own a box here, the six essential ones and 19 others;
%% three more (visualizer3d, ray_trace, solar) are named inside a caption
%% without owning a box of their own. Modules entered: a default area run
%% (no --download, no --shard) runs a function of 16 package modules, and
%% four of those sixteen are named nowhere in this drawing - cli_common,
%% run_utils, classes_config and viz_common.
%% Every box names what it draws in qualified form: module.function,
%% module.Class.method or module.Class. Three boxes are not one name: M13 and
%% C21 each hold the functions of one call chain, and R6 is a repository
%% script path. No source line numbers, they rot.
%% Arrows carry the data that crosses them: in-memory objects, or file names.
%% Read side (decoder, rays, solar) is drawn apart: it never builds a store.
%% The init block and the one invisible link "P1 ~~~ P2" are layout only: they
%% stack the two panels instead of letting dagre put them side by side, which
%% takes the render from 10205 px wide to 6229. The invisible link is the only
%% edge here without a label, and it carries no arrow head and no line.
%% Render:
%%   npx -y @mermaid-js/mermaid-cli -i 12_workflow_schema.mmd -o 12_workflow_schema.svg -b white
%%{init: {"flowchart": {"nodeSpacing": 18, "rankSpacing": 40, "wrappingWidth": 200, "padding": 5}}}%%
flowchart TB
    LEGEND["<b>Legend</b><br>solid arrow = call on the default path, with the data it passes<br>thick arrow = data hand-off: neither box calls the other,<br>their common caller passes the value the label names<br>dashed arrow = branch taken only under the flag on the label<br>arrow out of a cylinder = a read of that file, not a call<br>arrow ending in a cross = the artifact is deleted there<br>parallelogram = input data<br>cylinder = artifact written to disk<br>dashed cylinder = intermediate, deleted on a clean exit<br>thick gold border = a function of one of the six essential modules:<br>data_structures, voxelize, decoder, sharding, merge_streaming, area_cli"]

